\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The spindle at squared radius $a$. Two shell points at distance $\sqrt  a$ are carried onto each other by $\theta _a$ and land at distance exactly $1$, which is what the rotation is for. Half that displacement has length $\genfrac  {}{}{}112$ always, since $\lvert 1-\theta _a\rvert = 1/\sqrt  a$; whether it lies in the lattice is the whole content of Theorem\nonbreakingspace \ref  {thm:law}. Drawn for $a=4$, $\qopname  \relax o{cos}\theta _4 = 7/8$.}}{4}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:spindle}{{1}{4}{The spindle at squared radius $a$. Two shell points at distance $\sqrt a$ are carried onto each other by $\theta _a$ and land at distance exactly $1$, which is what the rotation is for. Half that displacement has length $\tfrac 12$ always, since $\lvert 1-\theta _a\rvert = 1/\sqrt a$; whether it lies in the lattice is the whole content of Theorem~\ref {thm:law}. Drawn for $a=4$, $\cos \theta _4 = 7/8$}{figure.1}{}}
