\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The entire interface of $\theta _4$: all $126$ cross edges a depth-$4$ ball offers, computed from the coordinates. Sixty run to the shared origin---the inner star---thirty join two points of the shell at radius $2$, and thirty-six run crosswise between the dotted radii $\genfrac  {}{}{}1{\sqrt  {11}}{2}\mp \genfrac  {}{}{}1{\sqrt  3}{6} = 1.3696$ and $1.9470$. Both halves here are the same ball. One edge of each non-trivial orbit is drawn heavy, its end in $L$ filled and its end in $\theta _4(L)$ open. Every edge in the figure has length exactly $1$.}}{13}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:orbits}{{3}{13}{The entire interface of $\theta _4$: all $126$ cross edges a depth-$4$ ball offers, computed from the coordinates. Sixty run to the shared origin---the inner star---thirty join two points of the shell at radius $2$, and thirty-six run crosswise between the dotted radii $\tfrac {\sqrt {11}}{2}\mp \tfrac {\sqrt 3}{6} = 1.3696$ and $1.9470$. Both halves here are the same ball. One edge of each non-trivial orbit is drawn heavy, its end in $L$ filled and its end in $\theta _4(L)$ open. Every edge in the figure has length exactly $1$}{figure.3}{}}
